  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 352 AM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nCentral CONUS upper-level ridging will continue to build eastward \ntoward the East Coast through midweek, while the stalled surface \nfrontal boundary over northern Florida gradually washes out. High \npressure over the western Atlantic is expected to re-establish full \ncontrol over the region by Wednesday. PWATs and convective coverage \nwill lower back toward seasonal normals. Convection will remain \nprimarily sea-breeze and mesoscale driven, with morning showers and \nisolated thunderstorms moving onshore along the East Coast before \nshifting inland and toward SW Florida during the afternoon and early \nevening. \n\nAnother mid/upper-level trough and associated surface frontal \nboundary are expected to dig south along the Eastern Seaboard by the \nend of the week, nudging the western Atlantic ridge axis southward \nonce again. Meanwhile, guidance also indicates a plume of Saharan \ndust could overspread the region sometime this weekend. Confidence \nremains low regarding the timing and impact of these features on \nlocal weather.\n\nHigh temperatures will rise into the lower 90s across most areas \nthrough the weekend, reaching the mid 90s over interior SW Florida. \nOvernight lows will range from the upper 70s/lower 80s along the \ncoasts to the low/mid 70s over the interior. NBM probabilities of \nheat indices exceeding 105 degrees generally increase toward the \nsecond half of the week, along with increasing probabilities of \nMajor HeatRisk probabilities across urban areas of South Florida.",
